[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was marked known, with one cause and one fix queued. The desk's own
cause rule disagreed with that page: run over the same reports, it sorts them into more than one
cause. Another problem was sitting in the desk's own notes, counted as a footnote.

The desk has since closed #4471. It fixed one of these problems, declined a second, and did not
mention the third.

<Callout kind="warn">
One problem in this ticket is still unexamined. It was never named, so it was never fixed and never
declined — it was merged into a ticket that has now been closed.
</Callout>

## What was in #4471

<ProblemSplit ticket={4471} />

Each row is a different bug with a different fix. The parser row was one change in one shared
place. The hint row was a change to one booking form, and the parser fix never touched it: those
people were typing month-first because the form told them to, and the form was reading them
day-first. The one-day row is not a month flip at all.

## What the desk did

#4471 closed on 5 October:

| problem | what happened |
|---|---|
| `parser` | fixed, by `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales` |
| `hint` | won't fix — booking-v3 was retired |
| `one-day` | not mentioned |

The first two are resolutions. The third is a gap: those reports were merged into #4471, #4471 is
closed, and nobody has looked at what they describe.

Note also what the shape of this says about the merge. The desk needed two different answers for
one ticket, and gave them — a fix and a withdrawal. A ticket that needs two answers was two
tickets.

## Why one ticket swallowed them

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges a report into an open ticket when its
subject line matches, lowercased and stripped of punctuation. Nobody reads the body. During an
incident morning that is the right trade, and the desk made it deliberately.

It cannot separate these problems, because the cause is not in the subject. The cause follows from
the tenant's locale and the booking form they run, and a reporter types neither. Two people can
write the same sentence and be hitting different bugs. The desk's own argued pairs show it:

<SubjectProof />

Decision 0001 predicted half of this: "Reports with different subjects about the same bug end up in
separate tickets." The reverse happened too, and nothing in the rule can catch it.

## Where the desk's table stops

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

That table is not wrong. It counts the one-day reports under the cause their tenant implies and
footnotes them, which is the written convention. But the footnote is the problem: those reports
describe a date that moved by a single day, which neither the parser bug nor the hint bug can
produce. A month flip moves 12 May to 5 December. These people are describing Tuesday showing as
Monday, one client in Lisbon and one in Toronto getting different days for the same meeting, and an
invite that arrived before the booking was made. That is a date rendered in the viewer's timezone
from a timestamp, not a date parsed in the wrong order.

The parser fix shipped. If reports like these keep arriving, this is why.

## Tickets

One ticket per problem, in
[sushiilearns/kb-4471-split](https://github.com/sushiilearns/kb-4471-split). Each one states the
reports it covers and how they were counted.

- [`parser`](https://github.com/sushiilearns/kb-4471-split/issues/1) — closed, fixed by the change
  that shipped with #4471.
- [`hint`](https://github.com/sushiilearns/kb-4471-split/issues/2) — closed as won't fix, because
  booking-v3 was retired. Nothing was repaired; the form carrying the bug was withdrawn.
- [`one-day`](https://github.com/sushiilearns/kb-4471-split/issues/3) — **open.** No fix, no
  diagnosis, and no decision from the desk.

## What `one-day` needs next

Start with how a stored date becomes a day for display, and in whose timezone — the tenant's, the
viewer's, or UTC. The Lisbon and Toronto report is the one to reproduce first: one meeting, two
readers, two different days.

## Before you rely on this page

- The one-day group is the desk's own list in `desk-notes.json`, read from the report bodies. If
  more reports describe a one-day move under a different subject, they are still counted under
  their tenant's cause here, and this page undercounts the group.
- `hint` and `parser` come from the desk's rule in `causes.ts`, not from my reading. If that rule
  is wrong about a tenant's form, these counts move with it.
- The counts are computed from the 4 October export. The desk's own resolutions above are later
  than that export, so the two were not produced from the same snapshot.

